    "report": "Hyphenated phone numbers are now recognised as contact details in both places that hold the rule, and the work is committed on `main` as `50c6e18`. Nothing was pushed and no server was started.\n\n**What changed**\n\n- **Messaging (`ContactDetails.ts`)**: the phone pattern is now `\\+?\\d(?:[ .-]?\\d){8,}`. Matches are held back as `[hidden]`, as before.\n- **Reviews (`ReviewRules.ts`)**: the same pattern. Matches are refused as `CONTACT_DETAILS`, as before. I also updated the doc comment to describe hyphens.\n- **Tests**: added hyphenated cases to both spec files. `06-12-34-56-78` and `+33-6-12-34-56-78` are now covered in each. The existing date assertions (`2026-12-01`) still pass.\n\n**Checks**\n\n- Unit tests: all 153 suites and 1030 tests pass. The run ignored my path filter and ran everything.\n- `yarn check:conventions`: passes.\n- ESLint and Prettier: clean on the four changed files. Prettier reformatted one spec file, which I included in the commit.\n- I did not run the e2e suite. The review e2e spec has no hyphenated digits, so I don't expect it to change.\n\n**Decisions and unclear points**\n\n- I changed the rule in both contexts rather than moving it to the shared kernel. ADR 0063 says a change to what counts as a phone number is made in each context that holds it, so I followed that.\n- The IBAN and email patterns are untouched, as you asked.\n- A date-like run of nine or more digits joined by hyphens would now count as a phone number. That is the consequence of the rule as written; `2026-12-01` has only eight digits, so it is not caught.\n- The ADRs only describe phone numbers in general terms, so I left them alone.",
